# Finite real-textarea correction review

The independent review used the exact frozen corrected client in an owned loopback HTTP bridge, together with its unchanged real list/read/search/save server handlers. `gitStatus` deliberately returned an uninitialized repository without invoking Git. The shared Studio host and original VS Code workbench were not used. Source-before/after binds eleven exact source files, the author852 payloads, and the bridge. All wire/capture observations were persisted incrementally; the host exited normally after authenticated shutdown and confined temporary-file removal.

Nine literal selections and seventeen regex selections passed in actual Chrome. The four saved fixtures contain LF, CRLF, bare CR and mixed endings, with astral characters before later matches. The literal substring is `needle`; the regex is `needle|😀`. The seventeen regex selections comprise nine words and eight complete astral characters. These are independently selected native observations, distinct from the author's twenty-two DOM-model groups and twenty-six model substrings.

The normalized textarea indices were eight through fourteen for each first word, thirty-one through thirty-seven for each second word, and forty-three through forty-nine for the third word in the mixed-ending file. The astral selections were zero through two and twenty-three through twenty-five. The saved-file source coordinate labels remain unchanged; a bare CR is not a saved LF-delimited line separator even though the native textarea displays it as LF. No changed line-label compatibility is claimed.

Two actual response controls passed. A held search response did not publish after changing the Whole word option: the result container remained empty. A held unopened-file read did not publish after accepting Close on the sole clean tab: the editor remained empty and disabled, with no tabs. Their held server states, response capture and release events are retained separately from the native DOM observations.

The next control edited a draft, held another unopened-file read, and attempted Close. The actual native confirmation was observed. The browser click and all supported dismissal/closure attempts timed out at browser focus control. No confirmation function was replaced. That control is incomplete, not a pass or a confirmed application defect. A Save-specific actual-browser control was not executed after the barrier. The prior independent sixteen groups were subsequently replayed against the correction with behavior bodies unchanged and explicit import/collector identity adapters; all sixteen passed. Their four DOM-model groups use the original unnormalized model and do not substitute for these native-browser checks.

The host's graceful shutdown and exact sources are verified. Closure of the owned browser tab is unconfirmed. Automatic ephemeral cleanup at the end of the turn is not counted as a verified close. No screenshot file is included. The original573 candidate, its prepared finite comparisons, and the independently observed negative141 review remain unchanged. The original six equal and two unequal groups were not re-executed or promoted by this DOM-only correction review.
